{"name":"Ambit","hex":"#97653f","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ambit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The extent of actions, thoughts, or the meaning of words, etc.","The boundary around a building, town, region, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ambit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ambit (magazine)","blocks":[{"text":"Ambit was a quarterly literary periodical published in the United Kingdom. The magazine was founded in 1959 by Martin Bax, a London novelist and consultant paediatrician."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c6/Ambit_%28magazine%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambit_(magazine)","wikidata":"Q4741504"},{"title":"AMBIT","blocks":[{"text":"AMBIT is a historical programming language that was introduced by Carlos Christensen of Massachusetts Computer Associates in 1964 for symbolic computation. \nThe language was influenced by ALGOL 60 and is an early example of a pattern matching language for manipulation of strings .\nThe acronym AMBIT stands for \"Algebraic Manipulation by Identity Translation\", but has also claimed \"Acronym May Be Ignored Totally\".\nAMBIT had dialects for manipulation of lists (AMBIT-L) and graphs (AMBIT-G) \nBoth pioneered with data structure diagrams and visual programming as data and patterns were used to be represented by directed-graph diagrams.\nAMBIT/L was implemented for a PDP-10 computer and used to implement the interactive algebraic manipulation system IAM."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AMBIT","wikidata":"Q4652541"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c6/Ambit_%28magazine%29.jpg/250px-Ambit_%28magazine%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c6/Ambit_%28magazine%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c6/Ambit_%28magazine%29.jpg/250px-Ambit_%28magazine%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmbit_(magazine).jpg"}],"sounds":{"ambit":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-I_learned_some_phrases-ambit.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/12/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-I_learned_some_phrases-ambit.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-I_learned_some_phrases-ambit.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/12/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-I_learned_some_phrases-ambit.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-I_learned_some_phrases-ambit.wav.ogg"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Sèvres - Magot - Pajou, buste de Madame du Barry 08","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/S%C3%A8vres_-_Magot_-_Pajou%2C_buste_de_Madame_du_Barry_08.jpg/500px-S%C3%A8vres_-_Magot_-_Pajou%2C_buste_de_Madame_du_Barry_08.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%A8vres_-_Magot_-_Pajou,_buste_de_Madame_du_Barry_08.jpg","share":0.195625},{"title":"Vase with lamps, Brown and beige textures, Rostov-on-Don, Russia","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Vase_with_lamps%2C_Brown_and_beige_textures%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg/500px-Vase_with_lamps%2C_Brown_and_beige_textures%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg","ratio":1.5049019607843137,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vase_with_lamps,_Brown_and_beige_textures,_Rostov-on-Don,_Russia.jpg","share":0.176875},{"title":"Brown Rat (Rattus norvegicus) also called Norway Rat or Common Rat -with food container - Mathias Baldwin Park, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, USA","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia